◆Residents&rsquo; Distrust, No Trust in Integration Talks

Distrust in politicians was also clear in resident interviews. One anonymous resident said, &ldquo;Integration may be necessary for the greater good, but in reality residents&rsquo; benefits will inevitably decline. Leaders only seem interested in saving face and protecting their positions.&rdquo;

Kim In-cheol (alias, 60s) likewise remarked, &ldquo;Integration could be a path for Jeonbuk&rsquo;s overall development, but unless residents are given credible explanations and leaders show responsibility, it will be hard to gain support. The problem is that politicians&rsquo; sincerity is not being conveyed.&rdquo;

Minister of the Interior and Safety Yoon Ho-jung recently visited Wanju, saying, &ldquo;A referendum can only happen if both sides agree procedurally. If conditions allow, six-party talks can also be held.&rdquo;

A provincial official added, &ldquo;All six parties are positive about the talks, and the schedule is currently being coordinated.&rdquo;

The Jeonju&ndash;Wanju integration debate can be summed up as a &ldquo;clash between principle and reality.&rdquo; Politicians and administrators stress the need for integration to promote regional development, attract businesses, and enhance national competitiveness. Residents, however, oppose it over concerns of taxes, benefits, daily living, and nuisance facilities.

Governor Kim&rsquo;s pledge to resolve &ldquo;unfinished tasks&rdquo; and Mayor Woo&rsquo;s explanation that &ldquo;debt is an asset investment&rdquo; are not convincing to residents because of the gap with lived reality. Coexistence without trust is nothing more than an empty promise. Unless political leaders first restore trust, an integration pursued only in the name of principle will deepen conflict rather than resolve it.

